	abstract = {As federated and multi-cloud ecosystems grow at a very high rate, ensuring secure and verifiable keyword search in data silos that are independently operated is a critical issue. This research is based on the Privacy-Preserving and Trusted Keyword Search for Multi-Tenancy Cloud (VAKSE and PVAKSE) framework and presents an Enhanced Federated and Explainable VAKSE (F-EVAKSE) one. The suggested framework combines a blockchain-based accountability layer with a quantum-resistant lattice encryption module to provide verifiable, auditable, and privacy that will last into the future. In addition, explainable verification metrics are also built in to identify abnormal or malicious query behaviors based on interpretable attention-weighted learning. Experiments performed on the Enron and Wikipedia datasets (517K and 9.2M documents, respectively) show that, on average (compared to PVAKSE), the encryption time has decreased by 37.8% and parallel search efficiency has increased by 42.5%, and the average latency to verify a token is 0.01 s. The suggested F-EVAKSE model has a verification accuracy of 99.3 percent, an accountability accuracy of 96.5 percent, and compensates less than 3.1 percent of computation costs in federated clouds. The findings show that F-EVAKSE offers scalable, transparent, quantum-resilient, and keyword search with high privacy guarantees that make it suitable for cross-tenant and cross-cloud deployment. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
